00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.470 align:start position:0%
this month the us senate confirmed  jerome powell as federal reserve chair  for a second term  it's a move that solidifies the central  bank's top leadership as it battles some  of the worst inflation in 40 years  it's a worry on the minds of everyone in  the financial system from everyday  families feeling pressure at the grocery  store or gas pump to fund managers and  multinational corporations and one of  its largest contributors is the shipping  business  the global supply chain mess related to  covet 19 lockdowns is far from over and  many companies are struggling with  paying for sky-high shipping rates  rates from asia to the west coast which  are home to america's busiest ports are  almost triple what they were last year  and 10 times what they were in 2020 the  going rate is about 16 000 per container  this has created a boom time for  shipping companies which raked in record  profits last year  maersk one of the world's largest  shippers said its profits exploded to 18  billion dollars in 2021  six times more than 2020.  the biden administration is now  targeting these shipping giants saying  the lack of competition has allowed them  to take advantage of soaring demand by  jacking up prices and thereby  contributing to inflation but it's not  just the shipping fees causing the pain  port fees are also cutting deeply into  company logistics which are also passed  on to the consumer  for example greg hayes the ceo of branch  a new york based office furniture  company told forbes they've had to spend  a few thousand dollars a day on port  fees and has paid about five hundred  thousand dollars in fees in the last  several months  hayes says he reluctantly raised prices  by 20 to accommodate these changes  so how can companies find a way out this  is where bringing manufacturing back  closer to consumers can help and  companies have been saying for some time  now that diversifying from chinese labor  would be beneficial but such a shift  will take time ambrose conroy the  founder of supply chain consulting firm  serif put it this way it was a labor  arbitrage play to manufacture in china  to do that logistics costs had to be low  now logistics costs are high and labor  costs are high the whole model has  broken  [Music]  [Music]
 

